faq:
  - q: "What are the strongest job markets in Florida in 2026?"
    a: "Florida has three distinct major job markets: (1) Miami — finance, tech, and international business. Miami has emerged as a significant financial hub, with Citadel's relocation from Chicago, growing private equity and hedge fund presence, and a tech startup ecosystem fueled by Latin American founders and international capital. Key employers: Carnival Corporation, Lennar, Chewy, Ryder, UHealth, and dozens of financial services firms. (2) Tampa Bay — healthcare, financial services, and professional services. Dominant employers: Moffitt Cancer Center, AdventHealth, Raymond James Financial, TD Bank US headquarters, WellCare, and USAA Florida operations. (3) Orlando — hospitality, aerospace, defense, and healthcare. Disney World, Universal Studios, Lockheed Martin, Boeing, AdventHealth, and a growing simulation and training technology sector (FAAC, CAE USA) make Orlando more economically diverse than its tourism reputation suggests."
  - q: "Why is Miami becoming a financial hub and what does it mean for job seekers?"
    a: "Miami's emergence as a financial center is driven by several factors: high-profile relocations (Citadel, Point72's growing presence, Millennium Management's Miami office), favorable Florida tax environment (no state income tax), lifestyle appeal for high earners escaping cold-weather markets, and Latin American capital seeking a US base. What this means for job seekers: (1) Finance roles in Miami are growing, particularly in asset management, private equity, family offices, and wealth management. (2) The talent pool is less deep than NYC or Chicago — meaning strong finance candidates have less competition for Miami finance roles than equivalent roles in those markets. (3) The network is smaller — Miami finance is a tight-knit community where personal connections matter enormously. (4) Compensation benchmarks are lower than NYC equivalents — Miami finance roles typically pay 15–25% less than comparable New York roles, partially offset by Florida's no-state-income-tax advantage."
  - q: "How does Florida's no-income-tax status affect job offer negotiation?"
    a: "Florida has no state income tax — the same advantage as Texas, Nevada, and a handful of other states. For high earners, this creates meaningful take-home pay advantages compared to California, New York, or Massachusetts. Key implications for negotiation: (1) When comparing a Florida offer to an offer in a high-tax state, compute after-federal-tax income for Florida vs. after-federal-plus-state income for the comparison state. At $200K income, Florida's no-state-income-tax can mean $15–25K+ in additional take-home relative to California or New York. (2) Florida's affordability advantage matters — cost of living in Miami, Tampa, and Orlando is materially lower than NYC, SF, or Boston, which further strengthens the real-value comparison for Florida offers. (3) When negotiating a Florida offer, the correct anchor is private-sector market rates for your role in Florida — not California or New York equivalents — adjusted for local cost of living."
  - q: "What should healthcare professionals know about the Florida job market?"
    a: "Florida is one of the largest healthcare markets in the US, driven by its large and growing older population. Key dynamics: (1) Massive demand — Florida consistently ranks among the highest states for healthcare job openings per capita, particularly for nurses, physicians, and allied health professionals. (2) Major health systems — AdventHealth (headquartered in Orlando), HCA Healthcare's Florida market, Moffitt Cancer Center, Baptist Health South Florida, and University of Miami Health System are among the state's largest employers. (3) Administrative and business roles — the scale of Florida's healthcare sector creates significant demand for health system administrators, revenue cycle professionals, and healthcare consultants, not just clinical roles. (4) Locum tenens and travel nursing — Florida's healthcare demand makes it one of the top markets for locum tenens physicians and travel nurses, with premium compensation packages for qualified candidates."
  - q: "What is the Miami tech startup scene like for job seekers?"
    a: "Miami's tech startup ecosystem has grown significantly since 2020, when prominent tech founders and VCs began relocating. Key characteristics: (1) Latin American focus — Miami's tech ecosystem has a strong Latin American founder and investor community; startups serving LATAM markets are disproportionately Miami-based. Companies like Kavak, Nuvei, and dLocal chose Miami as their US base. (2) Stage is earlier — Miami startups are predominantly seed to Series B; the late-stage unicorn density of SF, NYC, and Boston does not exist in Miami. This means more risk but more equity upside and earlier responsibility. (3) Funding access is improving — Founders Fund, Andreessen Horowitz, and Tiger Global have invested in Miami companies; local VCs include Starlight Ventures, Rokk3r, and Medina Capital. (4) Compensation is lower than coastal markets — Miami startup compensation is typically 20–30% below SF equivalent roles at the same stage, partially offset by no state income tax and lower cost of living."
